# What a CLI upload (`vercel deploy`) must not send.
#
# Vercel's GIT integration honors .gitignore; a CLI upload does NOT — without
# this file the first `vercel deploy` from this repo tried to upload 4.8 GB of
# node_modules and build output (observed 2026-09-07, Phase 2 task 7).
#
# The CLI upload is the ONLY way to get a PREVIEW deployment of `main` here:
# a Git-sourced deployment of the production branch is a PRODUCTION deployment
# on Vercel regardless of `target` (plan-hosted-early-access.md, Phase 2's
# execution record), so previews come from a branch or from this path.
#
# Everything in the first block is either reinstalled (node_modules), rebuilt
# (.next, .turbo, dist) or local-only (Docker volumes, coverage). The second
# block is the secrets, and it is the half that actually needs stating.
node_modules
.next
.turbo
dist
coverage
.git
supabase/.branches
supabase/.temp
*.log

# SECRETS. The reason this section is not "obviously unnecessary": a CLI upload
# does not honor .gitignore (see above), so every file .gitignore keeps out of
# the repo is still sitting in this working tree, and `vercel deploy` would
# send it. `.env.ops` alone holds the PROD database password, the Supabase
# access token, the Vercel token and CRON_SECRET (D10); `supabase/.env` holds
# both Google client secrets; `client_secret*.json` is the raw download of one.
# None of them is read by a build — the app's own env comes from Vercel's env
# store — so there is nothing to lose by refusing to upload them.
#
# `.env.*` is a deliberate blanket: these patterns follow .gitignore matching,
# so it also covers a future `apps/web/.env.production`. That is correct here —
# D10 puts the app's build-time env in Vercel's own env store, never in a file
# — but it is the one line to revisit if a build ever needs to READ an env
# file rather than be handed the values.
.env
.env.*
supabase/.env
client_secret*.json
